;;; A rule's false `neq` conclusion is stored as a fact — M1f **W30**,
;;; register row `W30/assert-neq-x-x` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked by
;;; S1f.5.0 (2026-11-08) from S1f.5.7's § Conclusions (G-21), filed `open` and
;;; owned by S1f.5.7, on the user's ruling of 2026-11-07 (Q-M1e2.26, G-21).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact `(p a)` and a rule concluding `(neq ?x ?x)` from `(p ?x)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A rule's conclusion of `neq` is checked, never stored (G-21). At root the
;;; rule concludes `(neq a a)`, which is false, so root is a contradiction,
;;; explained as `(false)` is: its core is `(p a)`, the premise the firing
;;; matched, and `refuted by` names `r`. R is empty.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The conclusion is stored: root holds `(neq a a :rule r)` (`ein saturate
;;; FILE --dump`), H is empty and R is {∅}, exit 0 (`ein solve FILE` and `ein
;;; oracle FILE` at `ec004a48`, the engine `916d97ac`'s, release and debug
;;; alike). Root's state file does not load: a fact line `(neq a a)` is a
;;; parse error, so `answer_round_trip.rs` and `answer_dump.rs` name this file
;;; until S1f.5.7 fixes W30.

(relation p Node :why "p holds of {?1}")
(p a)
(rule r () :match (p ?x) :assert (neq ?x ?x) :why "p holds of {?x}, so {?x} differs from itself, which cannot be")
